    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The French founded New Orleans in 1718 on a natural levee beside a portage used by Native Americans, and it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1763, briefly back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and a large domestic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> made it the wealthiest and third most populous city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> by 1840, while free people of color, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a>, Irish, Germans and others gave it a mixed population and culture.</li><li>Fires, yellow fever, floods and hurricanes struck the city repeatedly, and in 2005 the failure of its levees in Hurricane Katrina flooded about 80 percent of it.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Choctaw name for the area, Bulbancha, has been translated as &quot;place of many tongues&quot;. Congo Square was a place where enslaved people were allowed to gather in public and play their native music.</p></aside>
    </section>

